      'The adaptive immune system generates its receptors by random rearrangement, so lymphocytes that recognize the body’s own molecules inevitably arise. Tolerance refers to the processes that keep such cells from causing harm. Central tolerance acts in the primary lymphoid organs, where lymphocytes mature. In the thymus, developing T cells are tested against complexes of self peptides and MHC molecules on thymic epithelial cells. Cells whose receptors fail to bind self MHC at all die by neglect; cells whose receptors bind weakly survive, a step called positive selection; and cells whose receptors bind self peptide–MHC strongly are driven into apoptosis, a step called negative selection. Because negative selection can eliminate only cells that encounter their antigen, epithelial cells of the thymic medulla express a transcription factor, AIRE, that drives the expression of hundreds of proteins otherwise restricted to particular tissues, such as insulin and thyroid proteins. A subset of self-reactive thymocytes are instead diverted into becoming regulatory T cells (Treg cells), which express the transcription factor FOXP3. Developing B cells in the bone marrow that bind self antigens strongly either rearrange a new light chain, a process called receptor editing, or are deleted.\n\nCentral tolerance is incomplete, so additional mechanisms operate in the periphery. A naive T cell is activated only when its receptor engages antigen on an antigen-presenting cell that also provides costimulatory signals, which are induced on these cells by microbial products and inflammation. A T cell that recognizes antigen in the absence of costimulation becomes anergic: it survives but cannot proliferate or produce effector cytokines on later stimulation. Treg cells suppress other lymphocytes that respond to the same antigen by consuming interleukin-2, by secreting inhibitory cytokines such as interleukin-10 and transforming growth factor β, and by reducing the costimulatory capacity of antigen-presenting cells. Some antigens, including those of the lens of the eye and the testis, are sequestered behind barriers and are normally never seen by lymphocytes. Finally, B cells specific for self proteins usually cannot be activated without help from a T cell specific for the same antigen, because antibody responses to protein antigens require that the B cell present peptides of the antigen to a helper T cell.\n\nAutoimmune disease results when these mechanisms fail. Inherited defects in AIRE or FOXP3 cause severe autoimmunity affecting many organs in early childhood. More commonly, disease depends on a combination of inherited susceptibility, particularly in MHC genes, and an environmental trigger. An infection can break tolerance by inducing costimulation in a tissue, so that self antigens there are presented to T cells under activating conditions. In molecular mimicry, an epitope of a microbe resembles a self epitope closely enough that lymphocytes activated by the microbe also react with the self molecule. Trauma can release sequestered antigens. The effector mechanisms of autoimmunity are those of a normal immune response: autoantibodies can activate complement or mark cells for destruction, can form circulating immune complexes that lodge in vessel walls, or can bind receptors and block or mimic the action of a hormone, while autoreactive T cells can directly destroy the cells of an organ, as occurs in the insulin-producing β cells of type 1 diabetes.',
