      'In a non-pregnant cycle, the corpus luteum that forms from the ruptured follicle secretes progesterone and estrogen under the stimulation of luteinizing hormone (LH). Progesterone maintains the secretory endometrium and suppresses uterine contractions. If fertilization does not occur, the corpus luteum regresses after about 12 days, progesterone falls, and the endometrium is shed. If the embryo implants, its outer trophoblast cells begin to secrete human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), a glycoprotein hormone whose β subunit differs from that of LH but which binds and activates the LH receptor. Maternal serum hCG is detectable about 8 days after fertilization and doubles roughly every 2 days in early pregnancy.\n\nAs the placenta develops, it becomes an endocrine organ in its own right. The syncytiotrophoblast synthesizes progesterone from maternal cholesterol. It also makes estrogens, but it lacks some of the enzymes needed to make them from cholesterol; instead it converts androgen precursors produced by the fetal adrenal gland, so that maternal estriol reflects the combined activity of the fetal adrenal and the placenta. Investigators measured maternal serum hormones and the diameter of the corpus luteum, by ultrasound, in 40 women with normal pregnancies at eight points in gestation (Table 1). They also reviewed an observational series of 30 women who had required removal of the ovary bearing the corpus luteum, most often for a twisted ovarian cyst, during pregnancy. Pregnancy continued in 1 of 10 women operated on before week 7, in 6 of 10 operated on during weeks 7 through 9, and in 10 of 10 operated on at week 10 or later. In a further 10 women operated on before week 7 who were given progesterone injections from the day of surgery, pregnancy continued in 9.\n\nThe fetal circulation differs from that after birth. Oxygenated blood from the placenta returns in the umbilical vein; much of it bypasses the liver through the ductus venosus and enters the inferior vena cava and the right atrium. Because the lungs are fluid-filled and their vessels are constricted by the low oxygen tension, pulmonary vascular resistance is high. Most inferior vena caval blood is directed across the foramen ovale into the left atrium and so reaches the coronary and carotid arteries first; most blood ejected by the right ventricle passes through the ductus arteriosus into the descending aorta, and only about a tenth of the combined ventricular output perfuses the lungs. Two umbilical arteries carry blood back to the placenta. At birth, expansion of the lungs with air lowers pulmonary vascular resistance, pulmonary blood flow rises, left atrial pressure rises above right atrial pressure, and the flap of the foramen ovale is pressed shut. The ductus arteriosus constricts over the first days of life in response to the rising arterial oxygen tension and the falling concentration of prostaglandins, which had kept it open before birth. Fetal hemoglobin, which binds 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate weakly, has a higher oxygen affinity than adult hemoglobin, favoring the transfer of oxygen from maternal to fetal blood in the placenta.',
